## 12. Security, Data Integrity, and Error Handling

Security must be preserved throughout every modification.

### 12.1 Authentication and authorization

- Preserve the existing authentication system.
- Preserve role-based routing and permissions.
- Enforce authorization on backend operations, not only in the UI.
- Do not grant additional privileges to make a feature easier to implement.
- Ensure protected routes remain protected.
- Prevent unauthorized users from modifying records, validations, inquiries, audit logs, or account details.

### 12.2 Supabase and database integrity

- Inspect existing tables, relationships, constraints, and RLS policies.
- Reuse existing tables and queries wherever possible.
- Create migrations only when genuinely necessary.
- Make migrations safe and compatible with existing data.
- Do not delete or overwrite production data.
- Do not expose service-role keys or other secrets in frontend code.
- Use the established Supabase client and service patterns.
- Ensure mutations handle errors and confirm success before updating the UI.

If database changes are required, provide the migration code and explain how it should be applied. Do not assume a migration has been executed against the live database merely because a migration file was created.

### 12.3 Loading, empty, and error states

Every affected feature should handle:

- Loading
- Successful data retrieval
- Empty results
- Validation errors
- Unauthorized access
- Backend or network errors
- Failed mutations

Use clear, understandable messages. Do not silently fail or display success when an operation did not complete.

